## 1. Objective and scope

Develop and test a state-space model explaining persistent tensile-strength differences among nominally identical 3D-printed polymer coupons.

The study will:

- Quantify variability attributable to feedstock, printer, build, spatial location, coupon geometry, post-processing, conditioning, and tensile testing.
- Separate persistent manufacturing states from transient disturbances and measurement error.
- Estimate which measurable factors predict latent coupon quality and tensile strength.
- Distinguish competing mechanisms, including:
  - variation in bulk material condition;
  - interlayer bonding differences;
  - porosity or dimensional variation;
  - thermal-history and cooling differences;
  - machine drift or calibration changes;
  - post-print aging or moisture effects;
  - testing-system artifacts.
- Assess both statistical association and causality through randomization, replication, blocking, controlled interventions, and independent measurements.

Primary endpoint: ultimate tensile strength. Secondary endpoints: elastic modulus, yield stress where defined, strain at break, fracture location, and fracture mode.

The scope is one polymer grade, coupon standard, and printing process initially. A preplanned confirmation phase will examine whether supported explanations generalize across additional material lots, printers, and production periods.

## 2. Assumptions

- “Nominally identical” means the same specified CAD geometry, slicing profile, material grade, orientation, process settings, post-processing protocol, conditioning, and tensile-test procedure.
- Actual manufacturing and testing states may depart from commanded settings.
- Coupon quality evolves through a partially observed dynamic process. For coupon \(i\), layer or time step \(t\):

\[
x_{i,t}=F x_{i,t-1}+B u_{i,t}+G z_{i,t}+w_{i,t}
\]

\[
m_{i,t}=H x_{i,t}+v_{i,t}
\]

\[
y_i=\alpha+C\,g(x_{i,1:T})+\beta^\top q_i+b_{\text{group}(i)}+\epsilon_i
\]

where:

- \(x_{i,t}\) is the latent manufacturing state, such as effective melt condition, bond quality, cooling state, moisture state, deposition stability, or geometric integrity;
- \(u_{i,t}\) contains commanded process inputs;
- \(z_{i,t}\) contains measured disturbances and environmental variables;
- \(m_{i,t}\) contains in-process sensor observations;
- \(g(\cdot)\) summarizes the state trajectory into coupon-level attributes;
- \(q_i\) contains post-processing and test-stage covariates;
- \(b_{\text{group}(i)}\) represents lot, printer, build, operator, fixture, or test-day effects;
- \(w\), \(v\), and \(\epsilon\) represent process, sensor, and outcome noise.

- The model may require discrete regimes, such as normal extrusion, partial under-extrusion, thermal instability, or test slippage. A switching or nonlinear state-space model will be used if justified by diagnostics.
- Coupon observations are not independent when they share material lots, builds, printers, locations, conditioning batches, or test sessions.
- Destructive tensile outcomes cannot be repeatedly measured on the same coupon; inference therefore relies on replicated and exchangeable coupons plus nondestructive measurements.
- Sensor synchronization, calibration, and traceability are prerequisites for mechanistic interpretation.
- No single sensor proxy will be treated as equivalent to true interlayer bond strength without validation.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: measurement qualification and baseline characterization

1. Define a frozen reference manufacturing and testing protocol.
2. Calibrate printers, environmental sensors, dimensional instruments, imaging systems, load cells, extensometers, and alignment checks.
3. Conduct a gauge repeatability and reproducibility study for:
   - coupon dimensions;
   - mass and density;
   - image-derived porosity;
   - tensile outcomes;
   - fracture classification.
4. Print repeated reference coupons across several builds, printers, operators, days, and material spools or lots.
5. Randomize coupon IDs and blind tensile-test operators to printer, build position, and process history where feasible.
6. Use this phase to estimate variance components, temporal correlation, sensor noise, and effect sizes for formal sample-size simulation.

### Phase B: randomized blocked manufacturing study

Use a crossed or partially crossed design with:

- multiple printers, including nominally identical units when available;
- multiple material lots and multiple spools within lot;
- multiple builds per printer and lot;
- coupons distributed across build-plate locations;
- repeated production over nonconsecutive days;
- balanced print order and randomized coupon placement;
- randomized allocation to conditioning and test order.

Within practical constraints, cross rather than confound printer, lot, day, operator, post-processing batch, and test session. Include reference coupons in each build and test session.

Record continuous time-indexed data throughout each print. Preserve the layer structure so each coupon has a state trajectory rather than only build-level averages.

### Phase C: causal perturbation study

Apply controlled, safe perturbations around the reference process. Candidate factors include:

- feedstock moisture or conditioning state;
- nozzle-temperature offset;
- bed or chamber temperature;
- extrusion multiplier or flow rate;
- print speed;
- cooling-fan schedule;
- layer height;
- raster orientation or inter-road overlap;
- material residence time and pause events;
- build-plate position;
- post-print conditioning time, temperature, and humidity;
- tensile-test alignment, grip pressure, and strain rate within permitted standards.

Use a randomized factorial or response-surface design. Apply perturbations singly and in selected combinations so competing mechanisms yield distinguishable predictions. Include sham changes in which settings are re-entered without physically changing the process to detect operator or restart effects.

Where possible, use within-build contrasts to control shared history. Avoid varying so many factors simultaneously that state transitions cannot be identified.

### Phase D: diagnostic validation and confirmation

- Select high-, medium-, and low-strength coupons using a predeclared sampling rule, not only extreme failures.
- Obtain independent evidence of porosity, bead geometry, crystallinity or thermal history where relevant, fracture morphology, and local defects.
- Fit the model on a training subset defined by complete builds or production periods.
- Evaluate it prospectively on held-out builds, printers, lots, and days.
- Repeat the most informative intervention in an independent confirmation run.
- If supported by the data, apply a targeted corrective intervention—for example, drying feedstock or stabilizing nozzle temperature—and test whether both the inferred state and tensile-strength distribution change as predicted.

### Competing explanations and discriminating observations

- **Material moisture or degradation:** strength should track measured moisture, drying history, residence time, and possibly spectroscopic or thermal indicators; controlled drying should improve the predicted latent state and strength across printers.
- **Thermal or interlayer-bond history:** strength should track layer-level temperature and cooling trajectories, show anisotropic or interfacial fracture, and respond to temperature, chamber, or fan interventions.
- **Porosity or under-extrusion:** strength should track mass deficit, density, internal void fraction, bead-width instability, and flow signals; flow correction should reduce voids and improve strength.
- **Printer calibration or drift:** residuals should cluster by printer or time and follow changes in measured machine state; recalibration should create a state change and reduce the printer effect.
- **Build-position effects:** spatially repeatable gradients should appear within builds after controlling for other variables and should move or disappear when airflow or coupon placement is changed.
- **Post-processing or conditioning:** strength should track time-humidity-temperature exposure after printing, and randomized conditioning interventions should shift outcomes without corresponding print-state changes.
- **Testing artifact:** strength differences should track frame, fixture, operator, alignment, extensometer, grip behavior, or test order; retesting reference materials or exchanging fixtures should transfer the apparent effect.
- **Unmeasured regime changes:** abrupt changes in residual behavior, sensor covariance, or strength distribution should support a switching-state model rather than a single continuous process.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

### Provenance and commanded settings

- Resin grade, supplier, lot, spool, storage duration, opening date, drying history, and reuse history.
- Printer ID, firmware, nozzle ID and wear history, maintenance and calibration records.
- CAD and slicer versions, toolpath, orientation, infill, shell count, layer height, raster sequence, temperatures, speeds, flow commands, cooling schedule, and pauses.
- Build date, start time, operator, build-plate preparation, coupon location and orientation.
- Post-processing, storage, conditioning, transport, and test history.

### Time-indexed manufacturing evidence

- Actual nozzle, bed, chamber, and local part temperatures.
- Ambient temperature and relative humidity.
- Extruder current or torque, commanded and actual feed rate, and filament motion if available.
- Printhead position, speed, acceleration, layer number, elapsed time, pauses, and fault logs.
- Images or video sufficient to identify deposition interruptions, warping, bead-width changes, and surface defects.
- Sensor timestamps linked to toolpath and coupon location.

### Coupon-level evidence

- Dimensions at predefined locations, mass, apparent density, warpage, surface roughness, and visual-defect scores.
- Nondestructive internal-defect or porosity measurements for all coupons if feasible, otherwise a randomized subset.
- Material moisture at relevant stages.
- Thermal, chemical, or structural characterization on a balanced subset where pertinent to the polymer.
- Tensile stress-strain curves, not only derived scalar outcomes.
- Fracture location, angle, interlayer versus intralayer classification, and specimen validity.
- Fractography or microscopy for a predeclared stratified subset.

### Testing-process evidence

- Machine, load-cell, extensometer, grip and fixture IDs.
- Calibration and verification results.
- Alignment, gauge length, grip pressure or torque, strain rate, test order, operator, laboratory environment, slippage indicators, and failure location.
- Reference-material results for each test session.

## 5. Measurement

- Use a recognized tensile-coupon geometry and test standard, documented in advance.
- Measure actual cross-sectional dimensions for each coupon and retain both nominal-area and measured-area stress calculations. Do not let dimensional correction obscure manufacturing mechanisms.
- Sample temperature, motion, and extrusion signals fast enough to capture layer and transient behavior. Synchronize all devices to a common clock and quantify synchronization error.
- Calibrate sensors before the study and verify drift during it.
- Record raw signals and all preprocessing steps. Retain missingness, clipping, dropouts, and sensor-quality flags.
- Derive state-relevant features at layer, region, and coupon levels, including:
  - temperature mean, variance, gradients, cooling rate, and time above relevant thresholds;
  - flow error, current or torque instability, interruptions, and residence time;
  - spatial and temporal deposition variability;
  - moisture exposure and conditioning dose;
  - porosity, mass deficit, bead geometry, and warpage.
- Use automated extraction where possible, followed by blinded quality review.
- Assess repeatability and inter-rater agreement for visual and fracture classifications.
- Predetermine invalid-test criteria, such as grip failure or failure outside the gauge section. Report invalidity as an outcome because it may itself reflect coupon or fixture differences.
- Preserve traceability from each tensile result to raw material, layer-level manufacturing records, post-processing history, and test metadata.

## 6. Analysis

### State-space modeling

Fit a hierarchical state-space model in which layer-level latent states evolve from commanded inputs, observed disturbances, and prior states. Candidate state components include:

- effective thermal/bonding state;
- extrusion or consolidation state;
- material-condition state;
- geometric or defect state;
- machine-stability state.

Map sensor streams to these latent states through explicit observation models. Map cumulative states to coupon properties and tensile outcomes through physically motivated summaries, such as the minimum bond-quality state in the gauge region, time-integrated under-extrusion, spatially weighted porosity, or weak-layer prevalence.

Account for:

- nonlinear relationships and interactions;
- printer-, lot-, build-, day-, and test-session-level effects;
- spatial correlation within the build plate;
- temporal autocorrelation and drift;
- heterogeneous residual variance;
- censoring or invalid tests;
- sensor measurement error and missing data.

Compare linear Gaussian, nonlinear, and switching-state formulations using held-out predictive performance and residual diagnostics, not fit statistics alone.

### Variance attribution

Estimate the proportion of outcome variance attributable to:

- material lot and spool;
- printer and nozzle;
- build and day;
- build-plate location;
- observed state trajectories;
- conditioning and post-processing;
- test system and operator;
- residual coupon-level variation;
- measurement error.

Report uncertainty intervals and avoid interpreting variance partitions as causal unless supported by randomization or intervention.

### Causal assessment

For randomized perturbations, estimate intention-to-treat effects and state-mediated effects. A proposed mechanism will be stronger if:

1. the intervention changes the relevant measured or latent state;
2. the state predicts coupon properties and strength;
3. the direction and timing match the mechanism;
4. competing states do not explain the change;
5. the effect replicates independently.

Mediation analyses will be treated cautiously because latent-state measurement error and exposure-induced confounding can bias estimates.

### Model criticism and competing explanations

Use:

- posterior or simulation-based predictive checks;
- residual plots by printer, lot, position, time, and test system;
- change-point and regime diagnostics;
- leave-one-build and leave-one-printer-out validation;
- negative controls, such as pre-print variables that should not predict strength after randomization;
- sensitivity analyses for missing sensors, dimensional corrections, invalid-test handling, and alternative state definitions.

Compare each explanation by its predeclared observable predictions. Prefer explanations that predict tensile outcomes across held-out conditions and correctly predict the consequences of targeted interventions.

### Power and multiplicity

Determine final replication through simulation using Phase A estimates. Power the study for:

- scientifically meaningful strength differences;
- key intervention effects;
- major variance components;
- mediation through state variables;
- out-of-sample discrimination among leading models.

Define primary contrasts in advance. Control multiplicity for secondary factor searches or use hierarchical shrinkage, while reporting effect sizes and uncertainty rather than significance alone.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Data collection will stop only after:

- the preplanned minimum number of independent printers, lots, builds, days, and coupons is completed;
- all primary randomized contrasts meet their planned replication;
- measurement-system performance remains within prespecified acceptance limits;
- an independent confirmation run is completed.

A mechanism will be considered supported if it:

- accounts for a prespecified practically meaningful share of strength variation or intervention effect;
- has an effect direction and temporal pattern consistent with prior predictions;
- improves held-out prediction beyond provenance and commanded settings alone;
- survives sensitivity analyses;
- responds to a targeted intervention as predicted;
- replicates in the confirmation phase.

A testing-artifact explanation will be favored if apparent manufacturing differences substantially disappear after controlling or exchanging test-system components, or if reference materials show matching session effects.

Multiple mechanisms may be retained if each has independent predictive and intervention evidence. If no candidate meets the support threshold, conclude that the measured state is insufficient rather than selecting the best-fitting explanation by default.

Use a blinded interim review only for safety, sensor failure, gross nonidentifiability, or sample-size re-estimation based on nuisance variance. Do not stop early merely because a nominal significance threshold is crossed.

## 8. Limitations

- Latent states are model constructs and may not uniquely correspond to physical mechanisms.
- Some states may be weakly identifiable when sensors are sparse, highly correlated, or indirectly related to bond quality.
- Destructive testing prevents repeated tensile measurements on the same coupon.
- High-resolution imaging and material characterization may be feasible only on subsets, creating verification and selection challenges.
- Controlled perturbations may not reproduce naturally occurring combinations of faults.
- Printer, polymer, geometry, orientation, and environmental specificity may limit generalization.
- Sensor installation can alter thermal or mechanical conditions.
- Randomization is constrained by warm-up, material changes, and continuous-build operation, leaving possible carryover effects.
- Post-treatment mediation estimates can remain confounded despite randomization of process settings.
- Fracture initiation may depend on rare local defects that aggregate state summaries fail to capture.
- A successful predictive model does not establish mechanism without independent measurements and replicated interventions.
- Standards-based tensile results may not generalize to service loading, fatigue, impact, or long-term environmental exposure.

